For the ATLAS tau search, the full 47-page published PDF was available. I read the abstract, model parameterization, detector and dataset descriptions, reconstruction/calibration and trigger excerpts, statistical model/systematics, results and conclusion; I did not independently inspect the raw collision data, auxiliary likelihood files, or every cited detector-performance paper. For the 2026 ATLAS EFT combination, the 65-page arXiv PDF was available and I read its abstract, input-analysis inventory, event-overlap treatment, SMEFT parameterization, likelihood/systematics, confidence-interval method, and results. I did not reprocess the HEPData or audit every original measurement's detector calibration. For the CMS 2026 combination, the 79-page v2 paper was available; I read the input list, operator parameterization, systematic-correlation section, likelihood/result summary and caveats, not the raw event samples or all original channel analyses. For CMS-PAS-SMP-25-002, only the official CERN/CMS public result page was retrievable during this pass; the full PAS remains an explicit source-retrieval obligation. These are research notes, not an independent re-analysis.

## Latest-source boundary and source records

The source check establishes that the ATLAS global EFT paper was publicly available as arXiv:2604.21670v2 on 10 June 2026 and published in July 2026; CMS published its seven-measurement global SMEFT interpretation in 2026; CMS posted PAS SMP-25-002 on 30 July 2026; ATLAS public news lists SUSY summary plots updated March 2026. The last plot document could not be extracted from CERN CDS in this pass, so it contributes only to the update inventory, not a quantitative claim. I make no claim about papers/results released after this checked frontier.

