{"id":"aqft-eft-matching-running","kind":"A","order":982,"items":[{"id":"def-aqft-threshold-matching-map","kind":"definition","domain":"physics","title":"Finite-order EFT threshold matching map","reference":"items/def-aqft-threshold-matching-map.md#Definition","design_state":"Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.","dependency_level":4,"deps":["def-aqft-model-and-scale-data","def-aqft-operator-basis-physical-data","def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-model-and-scale-data":"formulation-prerequisite","def-aqft-operator-basis-physical-data":"formulation-prerequisite","def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ and Minkowski signature $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-,+,+,+)$. Fix a reference renormalization scale $\\mu_0>0$ and a common open interval $J\\subset(0,\\infty)$ of renormalization scales $\\mu$, with $\\mu_0\\in J$. Write $t=\\log(\\mu/\\mu_0)$, so $t$ is dimensionless. A threshold datum consists of a high-energy description, denoted $+$, and a lower-energy effective description, denoted $-$. The descriptions have a common declared light-field sector and differ by the degrees of freedom retained across a specified threshold of mass $M>0$. The external-energy domain and the range of validity of the local expansion are supplied separately; in a heavy-particle expansion they ordinarily require external energy $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}<M$. The Wilsonian matching scale $\\mu_W$, the renormalization scale $\\mu$, the threshold mass $M$, the external-energy bound $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, and the regulator cutoff $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ (denoted $\\Lambda$ in the finite-regulator model data) have mass dimension one and are distinct data; no equality between $\\mu_W$ and $\\mu$ is part of this definition.\n\nFor each sign $\\sigma\\in\\{+,-\\}$, supply a finite coefficient-coordinate space $\\mathbb C^{N_\\sigma}$ with $N_\\sigma\\ge1$, a declared ordered operator basis and power-counting truncation, and a renormalization-scheme coordinate map. Any identification of the Wilsonian coefficient data at $\\mu_W$ with these scheme coordinates is supplied input; this definition does not identify $\\mu_W$ with $\\mu$. The vector $C_\\sigma(t)\\in\\mathbb C^{N_\\sigma}$ is the column of dimensionless Wilson coefficients in that basis. The corresponding finite-order operator expression uses the supplied convention $C_{\\sigma,i}O_{\\sigma,i}/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op},\\sigma}^{d_i-4}$, with each $O_{\\sigma,i}$ of engineering dimension $d_i$ and $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op},\\sigma}>0$ the coefficient-suppression scale for that theory, denoted $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}$ in its physical operator contract and $\\Lambda$ in the generic scheme convention. It is distinct from $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{reg}}$, $M$, $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, $\\mu_W$, and $\\mu$ unless additional model data identify some of them. Write $\\rho_{N_\\sigma}$ for the realification that lists the real and imaginary parts of each coefficient. A supplied RG vector field is a continuous map $\\beta_\\sigma:\\Omega_\\sigma\\to\\mathbb R^{2N_\\sigma}$ on a supplied open domain $\\Omega_\\sigma\\subset J\\times\\mathbb R^{2N_\\sigma}$; an RG trajectory satisfies\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\rho_{N_\\sigma}(C_\\sigma(t))=\\beta_\\sigma(t,\\rho_{N_\\sigma}(C_\\sigma(t))).$$\nFor each sign, the physical operator datum is an instance of the selected physical EFT operator contract. It specifies finitely many field species and jet generators, finite-dimensional graded field spaces, smooth field domains and regularity, reality data, and rational engineering weights. Even fields are smooth maps; odd components take values in a fixed Grassmann algebra with smooth coefficient functions.\n\nThe Lorentz action is that of the proper orthochronous group\n$SO^+(1,3)=\\{L\\in GL_4(\\mathbb R):L^T\\eta L=\\eta,\\ \\det L=1,\\ L^0{}_0\\ge1\\}$,\nand each Lagrangian density is a Lorentz scalar. The datum gives the full gauge\nand flavor actions on fields, indices, and spurions. A spurion is a fixed\nnon-dynamical parameter used to record a declared symmetry-breaking insertion;\nabsent flavor symmetry is recorded as trivial, and broken symmetry requires\nits spurions and weights.\n\nThe datum fixes finite derivative, field-degree, dimension, and loop cutoffs,\nthe relation subspace and representatives, and a complex reductive affine\nalgebraic group with maps relating its representations to the physical gauge\nand flavor groups. Here reductive means there is no nontrivial closed normal\nunipotent subgroup; unipotent means every nonzero finite-dimensional rational\nmodule has a nonzero fixed vector. On each retained jet space the data either\nverify that the algebraic invariant subspace equals the intended physical\ninvariant space or label the quotient as a formal algebraic selection only.\n\nEach relation generator is recorded as a polynomial or Fierz identity, a total\ndivergence with compact-support or stated boundary conditions, or a\nleading-equation/field-redefinition relation tied to a base action and retained\norder. The latter records its variational derivative, multiplier, inverse\nthrough the retained order, and induced higher-order terms. This quotient alone\ndoes not establish action, off-shell Green-function, quantum-measure, or\nscattering equivalence.\n\nIf a non-Abelian gauge sector is selected, its actual compact real global group\n(compact meaning every open cover has a finite subcover), smooth right\nprincipal bundle (with smooth free and transitive right action and equivariant\nlocal trivializations), admitted bundle sectors, and unitary representations\n(actions preserving specified positive-definite Hermitian forms) are part of\nthe data. A selected smooth principal connection uses the right-action\nconvention $Z_P(p):=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_0p\\exp_G(tZ)$,\n$\\omega_p(Z_P(p))=Z$, and $R_g(p)=pg$,\n$R_g^*\\omega=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\\omega$, where\n$\\operatorname{Ad}_g=d(C_g)_e$ and $C_g(h)=ghg^{-1}$. Locally, for\n$A=s^*\\omega$ and a representation $\\rho$ with $\\rho_*:=d\\rho_e$, its\ncurvature and associated covariant derivative are\n$F_A=dA+\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A]$ and\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+\\rho_*(A_\\mu)$, using the supplied graded Lie-bracket\nconvention. Each finite basis represents only this supplied fixed-order\nquotient; no completeness claim about all physical relations or an infinite\noperator tower is made. These field and counting inputs are not inferred from\ncoefficient coordinates.\nFor $x\\in\\mathbb R^{2N_\\sigma}$, write $\\Phi_\\sigma(t,s)x\\in\\mathbb R^{2N_\\sigma}$ for the value at $t$ of the unique realified coefficient solution with value $x$ at $s$, whenever a unique such solution exists on the interval between $s$ and $t$.\n\nA declared matching calculation supplies a finite list of renormalized amplitudes or Green-function values $A_{\\sigma,j}(t,c;\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IR}})$, where $j$ indexes the selected external states and kinematics, $c$ is a coefficient vector, and $\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IR}}$ denotes the common infrared regulator used in both descriptions. Let $U_\\sigma\\subset\\mathbb C^{N_\\sigma}$ be open coefficient domains for $\\sigma\\in\\{+,-\\}$. The supplied matching family is a jointly continuously differentiable real map $\\widehat{\\mathcal M}:\\mathcal V\\to\\rho_{N_-}(U_-)$ on an open domain $\\mathcal V\\subset J\\times\\rho_{N_+}(U_+)$. Its admissible domain is the open subset $\\mathcal U=\\{(t,x)\\in\\mathcal V:(t,x)\\in\\Omega_+,\\ (t,\\widehat{\\mathcal M}(t,x))\\in\\Omega_-\\}$. It defines $M_t(c):=\\rho_{N_-}^{-1}(\\widehat{\\mathcal M}(t,\\rho_{N_+}(c)))$ for each admitted $(t,c)$, and $\\widehat M_t(x):=\\widehat{\\mathcal M}(t,x)$ on the corresponding slice. Thus the scale derivative and coefficient derivative in the compatibility condition below both exist and are continuous. It is a matching map through the declared loop order, operator dimension, and mass-expansion order when, for every selected $j$ and every admitted pair $(t,c)$ with $c\\in U_+$, the prescribed truncations of $A_{+,j}(t,c;\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IR}})$ and $A_{-,j}(t,M_t(c);\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IR}})$ agree. The truncation projection and its retained orders are part of the datum. This equality records only the selected amplitudes and orders; it neither estimates discarded terms nor asserts that a matching calculation exists for every model.\n\nA matching map is RG-compatible on these domains when, in realified coefficient coordinates,\n$$\\partial_t\\widehat M_t(x)+D_x\\widehat M_t(x)\\,\\beta_+(t,x)=\\beta_-\\!\\left(t,\\widehat M_t(x)\\right)$$\nfor every admitted $(t,x)$. Here $D_x$ is the ordinary real derivative with respect to the coefficient coordinates. RG-compatibility is an additional condition to be checked from the supplied beta functions and matching calculation; it does not follow merely from defining $M_t$ by amplitude equality.\n\nFor several thresholds, let $t_1>\\cdots>t_k$ be their logarithmic scales, let $\\Phi_j$ be the realified evolution in the theory active between thresholds $t_j$ and $t_{j+1}$, and let $\\widehat M_j(t_j)$ map realified coefficient data from the theory above threshold $j$ to the theory below it. The ordered high-to-low transport is\n$$\\Phi_k(t_f,t_k)\\circ\\widehat M_k(t_k)\\circ\\Phi_{k-1}(t_k,t_{k-1})\\circ\\cdots\\circ\\widehat M_1(t_1)\\circ\\Phi_0(t_1,t_i),$$\nwhenever all displayed flows and matching maps are defined. This definition specifies supplied finite-order data and their conventions; it computes no beta-function entry or matching coefficient and asserts no continuum limit.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Aneesh V. Manohar, Introduction to Effective Field Theories (full Les Houches lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.05863","locator":"Chapter 5, §§5.5–5.7 and §5.10; Chapter 7, §§7.1–7.2."},{"title":"C. P. Burgess, Introduction to Effective Field Theory (full review)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0701053","locator":"§§2.2.1–2.2.2 on scale cancellation and matching; §4.1.4 on charged-particle thresholds and decoupling-subtraction matching."}]},"library":"physics","physical_scope":"Finite-dimensional, fixed-order EFT coefficient and amplitude data with supplied threshold and RG maps; this definition asserts no generic continuum QFT or universal matching calculation."},{"id":"pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence","kind":"physics-theorem","domain":"physics","title":"Scale independence of compatible EFT matching and running","reference":"items/pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence.md#Statement","design_state":"Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.","dependency_level":5,"deps":["def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients","def-aqft-threshold-matching-map","thm-aqft-truncated-coupled-rge-wellposedness"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients":"mathematical-premise","def-aqft-threshold-matching-map":"formulation-prerequisite","thm-aqft-truncated-coupled-rge-wellposedness":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Let $K=[t_i,t_f]$ be a compact interval contained in the common open logarithmic-scale domains of a finite-order high-energy EFT and its lower-energy EFT. Use the realified coefficient coordinates, RG vector fields $\\beta_+$ and $\\beta_-$, flow maps $\\Phi_\\pm$, and realified matching map $\\widehat M_t$ of the finite-order threshold-matching definition. Assume each beta field is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the coefficient coordinates, locally uniformly in scale, and assume every flow used below exists on $K$. Assume the high-energy trajectory remains in the matching-family domain throughout $K$ and its matched image lies in the low-energy beta-field domain. Assume the matching family is jointly continuously differentiable in scale and coefficient coordinates and RG-compatible throughout these domains. For an initial high-energy realified coefficient vector $x_i$ with $(t_i,x_i)\\in\\Omega_+$ and any matching scale $s\\in K$, define the low-energy vector at the fixed final scale by\n$$x_-^{(s)}(t_f):=\\Phi_-\\!\\left(t_f,s\\right)\\!\\left(\\widehat M_s\\!\\left(\\Phi_+(s,t_i)x_i\\right)\\right).$$\nThen $x_-^{(s)}(t_f)$ is independent of $s$. Consequently, each selected renormalized low-energy amplitude or Green-function value, evaluated at the same final scale, external states, kinematics, infrared regulator, and operator truncation, is independent of the intermediate matching scale through the retained orders. The matching definition makes that low-energy amplitude agree with its selected high-energy counterpart through those same orders. The same conclusion holds for any finite ordered sequence of thresholds when each matching map satisfies the stated compatibility condition and the required flows exist. Contributions beyond the retained loop order, operator dimension, and mass expansion remain separate theoretical uncertainties; this result assigns them no numerical bounds. This is a conditional deduction from the supplied RG and matching data; it neither calculates beta functions or matching coefficients nor establishes a continuum QFT.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Aneesh V. Manohar, Introduction to Effective Field Theories (full Les Houches lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.05863","locator":"Chapter 5, §§5.5–5.7 and §5.10, on matching contributions, scale separation, RG evolution, and operator mixing."},{"title":"C. P. Burgess, Introduction to Effective Field Theory (full review)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0701053","locator":"§§2.2.1–2.2.2 on cancellation of intermediate-scale dependence and §4.1.4 on matching at charged-particle thresholds."}]},"library":"physics","physical_scope":"Conditional scale independence for a declared finite-order EFT pair with supplied locally Lipschitz RG fields, matching maps, and common renormalized amplitudes; no generic continuum or coefficient-calculation claim.","proof_strategy":"direct"}],"requires":["aqft-eft-operators","qftx-eft-rg-data"]}
{"id":"aqft-eft-matching-running-examples","kind":"B","order":982,"items":[{"id":"ex-aqft-qed-threshold-matching","kind":"example","domain":"physics","title":"One-loop electron-muon QED threshold matching and running","reference":"items/ex-aqft-qed-threshold-matching.md#Example","design_state":"Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.","dependency_level":5,"deps":["def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients","def-aqft-threshold-matching-map"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients":"mathematical-premise","def-aqft-threshold-matching-map":"formulation-prerequisite"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ and Minkowski signature $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-,+,+,+)$. Take QED with a real one-form photon potential $A=A_\\mu dx^\\mu$ on $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, field strength $F=dA$ with components $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, and two Dirac fields $\\psi_e,\\psi_\\mu$, each a four-component complex spinor-valued field, of charge $-e$ and positive masses $m_e<M_\\mu$. Use the minimal-coupling convention $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ieA_\\mu$ for charge $-e$, and the gauge kinetic term $-\\tfrac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}$. Define the dimensionless running coupling by $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)=e_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)^2/(4\\pi)$. The decoupling-subtraction (DS) convention uses minimal subtraction within each interval of active charged fields, removes a charged field from the low-energy theory below its threshold, and matches the coupling continuously at that threshold. Set $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(m_e)=\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}>0$ as the low-energy boundary condition. The source-reported one-loop DS flow has two active Dirac fields for $\\mu>M_\\mu$, one for $m_e<\\mu<M_\\mu$, and none for $\\mu<m_e$. If the one-loop solution remains in its positive-coupling domain, then for positive evaluation scale $Q$ of mass dimension one,\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(Q)}=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{Q^2}{m_e^2}\\right)\\qquad(m_e<Q<M_\\mu),$$\nand\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(Q)}=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{M_\\mu^2}{m_e^2}\\right)-\\frac{2}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{Q^2}{M_\\mu^2}\\right)\\qquad(Q>M_\\mu).$$\nThe two expressions agree continuously at $Q=M_\\mu$. These are solutions of the supplied one-loop renormalization-group equations; they are not measured values, exact all-orders predictions, or numerical error estimates.","provenance":{"statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"C. P. Burgess, Introduction to Effective Field Theory (full review)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0701053","locator":"§4.1.4, equations (81)–(86), the two-flavor QED model, matching at the muon threshold, and the decoupling-subtraction running."},{"title":"Aneesh V. Manohar, Introduction to Effective Field Theories (full Les Houches lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.05863","locator":"Chapter 7, §§7.1–7.2, equations (7.1)–(7.8), the one-loop fermion contribution to QED running and low-energy decoupling."}]},"library":"physics","physical_scope":"The source-reported one-loop decoupling-subtraction running of the electromagnetic coupling in QED with an electron and a muon; no experimental value, all-orders result, or model-independent threshold claim.","proof_strategy":"direct"}],"requires":["aqft-eft-matching-running"]}
