{"id":"aqft-smeft-heft","kind":"A","order":983,"items":[{"id":"post-aqft-smeft-model","kind":"postulate","domain":"physics","library":"physics","title":"Linearly realized Standard Model EFT field and scale data","reference":"items/post-aqft-smeft-model.md#Postulate","design_state":"Adopts the linearly realized three-family Standard Model field and gauge data and a finite B- and L-conserving canonical-dimension-six local model, with explicit scales and physical limits.","dependency_level":4,"deps":["def-countable-choice","def-aqft-operator-basis-physical-data","def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data","def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative","lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi"],"dependency_roles":{"def-countable-choice":"mathematical-premise","def-aqft-operator-basis-physical-data":"formulation-prerequisite","def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data":"formulation-prerequisite","def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative":"formulation-prerequisite","lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi":"formulation-prerequisite"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Adopt four-dimensional affine Minkowski spacetime $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with coordinates $x^0=ct$ and metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ for engineering dimensions, so $\\partial_\\mu$ has mass dimension one, a scalar field has dimension one, a spinor field has dimension $3/2$, and a gauge field strength has dimension two.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"B. Grzadkowski, M. Iskrzynski, M. Misiak and J. Rosiek, Dimension-Six Terms in the Standard Model Lagrangian","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1008.4884","locator":"§§1–3, printed pp. 1–5: the SM gauge group, field representations, natural field dimensions, canonical-dimension expansion and dimension-six coefficient convention."},{"title":"Adam Falkowski, Lectures on SMEFT","url":"https://scoap3-prod-backend.s3.cern.ch/media/files/79196/10.1140/epjc/s10052-023-11821-3_a.pdf","locator":"§2 and Table 1, printed pp. 5–8: assumptions behind SMEFT and the SM field representations; §§3.1–3.4, printed pp. 7–14: canonical-dimension power counting and dimension-six operators."}]}},{"id":"post-aqft-heft-model","kind":"postulate","domain":"physics","library":"physics","title":"Nonlinear electroweak Goldstone and scalar-singlet EFT data","reference":"items/post-aqft-heft-model.md#Postulate","design_state":"Adopts the nonlinear SU(2)-valued Goldstone field and independent scalar singlet with the exact right hypercharge action and separate HEFT scales.","dependency_level":5,"deps":["post-aqft-smeft-model"],"dependency_roles":{"post-aqft-smeft-model":"formulation-prerequisite"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Use the same four-dimensional Minkowski spacetime, direct-product global gauge group, gauge connection, fermion representations, family content, and the preceding model's exact baryon- and lepton-number selection rules on formal local interactions. No anomaly-free quantum-current claim is made. In particular, the hypercharge-one-half Higgs doublet of that model is replaced in this realization by three angular Goldstone coordinates and a real scalar singlet; this is a distinct adopted model interface, not a deduction that the two models have identical field spaces.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Gerhard Buchalla, Oscar Catà and Claudius Krause, Complete Electroweak Chiral Lagrangian with a Light Higgs at NLO","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1307.5017","locator":"§2, printed pp. 2–3, equations (1)–(9): nonlinear Goldstone matrix, singlet h, transformation law and leading scalar Lagrangian; Appendix A, printed pp. 22–23, arbitrary functions of h/v."},{"title":"Rodrigo Alonso, A Primer on Higgs Effective Field Theory with Geometry","url":"https://www.ippp.dur.ac.uk/~ralonso/PoHEFT.pdf","locator":"§3.2, printed pp. 14–15, HEFT building blocks and U/h transformation roles; §4, printed pp. 15–17, the HEFT expansion and separate scale."}]}},{"id":"def-aqft-warsaw-basis-contract","kind":"definition","domain":"physics","library":"physics","title":"Finite dimension-six Warsaw coefficient contract","reference":"items/def-aqft-warsaw-basis-contract.md#Definition","design_state":"Defines the source-listed B- and L-conserving dimension-six Warsaw coefficient representatives and their family and conjugation conventions without claiming a local classification proof.","dependency_level":5,"deps":["post-aqft-smeft-model","def-aqft-operator-basis-physical-data","def-aqft-operator-basis-quotient","def-axiom-of-choice"],"dependency_roles":{"post-aqft-smeft-model":"physical-assumption","def-aqft-operator-basis-physical-data":"formulation-prerequisite","def-aqft-operator-basis-quotient":"formulation-prerequisite","def-axiom-of-choice":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Use the fields, direct-product gauge group, hypercharge normalization, three-family content, exact $U(1)_B\\times U(1)_L$ symmetries, natural units and coefficient scale of the preceding linearly realized model. A local operator is a finite polynomial in these fields and their covariant derivatives at one spacetime point, with all Lorentz, gauge and family indices contracted to a Lorentz and gauge scalar. Its canonical dimension assigns one to $H$ and each derivative, $3/2$ to a fermion, and two to a field strength.","provenance":{"statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"B. Grzadkowski, M. Iskrzynski, M. Misiak and J. Rosiek, Dimension-Six Terms in the Standard Model Lagrangian","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1008.4884","locator":"§2 and Table 1, printed pp. 2–3, for field, hypercharge, index and sign conventions; §§3 and 5–7, printed pp. 4–14, for the B-conserving operator representatives and their stated EOM, integration-by-parts and Fierz reductions."},{"title":"Adam Falkowski, Lectures on SMEFT","url":"https://scoap3-prod-backend.s3.cern.ch/media/files/79196/10.1140/epjc/s10052-023-11821-3_a.pdf","locator":"Table 1 and §§3.4–4, printed pp. 8–20, for the three-family dimension-six Warsaw coefficient convention, basis nonuniqueness and field-redefinition/EOM caveat."}]}},{"id":"def-aqft-chiral-power-counting","kind":"definition","domain":"physics","library":"physics","title":"Finite electroweak HEFT chiral and loop counting","reference":"items/def-aqft-chiral-power-counting.md#Definition","design_state":"Defines the source-convention HEFT chiral and loop grading and finite NLO classes separately from canonical-dimension counting.","dependency_level":6,"deps":["post-aqft-heft-model","def-aqft-local-operator-and-dimension"],"dependency_roles":{"post-aqft-heft-model":"formulation-prerequisite","def-aqft-local-operator-and-dimension":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Use the HEFT fields $U$, $h$, gauge curvatures, fermions and arbitrary smooth coefficient functions of $h/v$ from the preceding HEFT model. The canonical engineering dimension $\\Delta$ is the mass weight of the local polynomial in fields and derivatives; it is the weight supplied by the finite-jet operator definition. The **chiral/loop count** is separate data and is not inferred from $\\Delta$.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Gerhard Buchalla, Oscar Catà and Claudius Krause, Complete Electroweak Chiral Lagrangian with a Light Higgs at NLO","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1307.5017","locator":"§3, printed pp. 3–4, equations (10)–(12), for the diagram degree and the finite one-loop counterterm classes; §§4.1–4.6, printed pp. 4–11, for the operator structures."},{"title":"Rodrigo Alonso, A Primer on Higgs Effective Field Theory with Geometry","url":"https://www.ippp.dur.ac.uk/~ralonso/PoHEFT.pdf","locator":"§§3.2–4, printed pp. 14–17, on the h/v functions, Goldstone building blocks and the distinction between canonical-dimension and HEFT expansions."},{"title":"Adam Falkowski, Lectures on SMEFT","url":"https://scoap3-prod-backend.s3.cern.ch/media/files/79196/10.1140/epjc/s10052-023-11821-3_a.pdf","locator":"§3.1, printed pp. 7–8, on canonical-dimension power counting for SMEFT, used here only to state the separate counting."}]}},{"id":"pthm-aqft-smeft-as-restricted-heft-branch","kind":"physics-theorem","domain":"physics","library":"physics","title":"The selected SMEFT model is a restricted HEFT branch","reference":"items/pthm-aqft-smeft-as-restricted-heft-branch.md#Statement","design_state":"Proves the smooth equivariant nonzero-doublet patch map and the finite polynomial and coefficient-image restrictions of the selected SMEFT branch.","dependency_level":7,"deps":["post-aqft-smeft-model","def-aqft-warsaw-basis-contract","post-aqft-heft-model","def-aqft-chiral-power-counting","def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative","lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi","def-axiom-of-choice"],"dependency_roles":{"post-aqft-smeft-model":"physical-assumption","def-aqft-warsaw-basis-contract":"formulation-prerequisite","post-aqft-heft-model":"physical-assumption","def-aqft-chiral-power-counting":"formulation-prerequisite","def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative":"mathematical-premise","lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi":"mathematical-premise","def-axiom-of-choice":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Let $v>0$, let $J=(-v,\\infty)$, and use the linearly transforming complex doublet $H$ with hypercharge $Y=1/2$ and the nonlinear fields $h\\in J$, $U\\in SU(2)$, with the gauge transformations and mostly-plus Minkowski conventions of the two adopted electroweak models. Here $\\sigma^2=\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{psmallmatrix}$ is the second Pauli matrix. Put $e_2=(0,1)^T$, $\\widetilde H=i\\sigma^2H^*$, and define","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"B. Grzadkowski, M. Iskrzynski, M. Misiak and J. Rosiek, Dimension-Six Terms in the Standard Model Lagrangian","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1008.4884","locator":"§§2–3 and Tables 1–3, printed pp. 2–5, for the field content, dimension-six source representatives, and coefficient convention."},{"title":"Gerhard Buchalla, Oscar Catà and Claudius Krause, Complete Electroweak Chiral Lagrangian with a Light Higgs at NLO","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1307.5017","locator":"§§2 and 5–6, printed pp. 2–3 and 12–15, for the nonlinear U,h convention and the distinction between the linear-Higgs branch and general HEFT functions."},{"title":"Rodrigo Alonso, A Primer on Higgs Effective Field Theory with Geometry","url":"https://www.ippp.dur.ac.uk/~ralonso/PoHEFT.pdf","locator":"§§3.1–3.2, 5.2 and 6, for the nonzero-doublet polar parametrization, scalar geometry and coefficient correlations; the local map below is proved directly."}]},"proof_strategy":"direct"}],"requires":["aqft-eft-operators","aqft-eft-matching-running","qftx-gauge-geometry"]}
{"id":"aqft-smeft-heft-examples","kind":"B","order":983,"items":[{"id":"ex-aqft-smeft-heft-observable-map","kind":"example","domain":"physics","library":"physics","title":"Tree-level scalar normalization and the charged-vector coupling","reference":"items/ex-aqft-smeft-heft-observable-map.md#Example","design_state":"Computes the local Q_HBox scalar kinetic coefficient and charged-vector coupling under the stated positivity and boundary assumptions.","dependency_level":8,"deps":["pthm-aqft-smeft-as-restricted-heft-branch","def-aqft-warsaw-basis-contract","def-aqft-chiral-power-counting","post-aqft-smeft-model","def-axiom-of-choice"],"dependency_roles":{"pthm-aqft-smeft-as-restricted-heft-branch":"physical-result","def-aqft-warsaw-basis-contract":"formulation-prerequisite","def-aqft-chiral-power-counting":"formulation-prerequisite","post-aqft-smeft-model":"physical-assumption","def-axiom-of-choice":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Use the selected mostly-plus, four-dimensional electroweak model with $v>0$, dimensionless gauge coupling $g>0$, and coefficient scale $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}>0$. Choose a real dimensionless coefficient $C_{H\\Box}$, set all other dimension-six coefficients to zero, and retain","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"B. Grzadkowski, M. Iskrzynski, M. Misiak and J. Rosiek, Dimension-Six Terms in the Standard Model Lagrangian","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1008.4884","locator":"§5, printed pp. 8–9, definition of Q_HBox and the scalar-sector representatives; §2, printed pp. 2–3, Higgs and gauge conventions."},{"title":"Adam Falkowski, Lectures on SMEFT","url":"https://scoap3-prod-backend.s3.cern.ch/media/files/79196/10.1140/epjc/s10052-023-11821-3_a.pdf","locator":"§4, printed pp. 15–20, basis changes and dimension-six Higgs interactions; used only as context for the stated finite coefficient choice."},{"title":"Rodrigo Alonso, A Primer on Higgs Effective Field Theory with Geometry","url":"https://www.ippp.dur.ac.uk/~ralonso/PoHEFT.pdf","locator":"§3.2, printed pp. 14–15, nonlinear scalar and Goldstone coordinates; the kinetic and coupling calculation below is derived directly."}]},"proof_strategy":"direct"}],"requires":["aqft-smeft-heft"]}
