 succeeded in 5592ms:
| `exp-aqft-cms-multiboson-smeft-2026` | CMS Preliminary PAS SMP-25-002 (30 Jul 2026): combination of ten 13 TeV Run 2 measurements (138 fb⁻¹) in VBS, VBF, diboson, and triboson channels; six CP-even bosonic Warsaw dimension-six operators; event-by-event LO-MC reweighting to detector level; binned profile likelihood, single/two/global profiling; the public report page says no significant deviation from the SM. | This is a preliminary report page. I could inspect its abstract, result summary, tables/figures landing page and cited methods, but the linked CDS PDF was behind an anti-bot interstitial. Treat the item as a candidate only until the full PAS is retrieved and its channels, event selections, detector calibration, nuisance correlations, coefficient conventions, and actual observed/expected intervals are checked. Do not copy numeric limits from search snippets. |
| `exp-aqft-atlas-tau-squark-gluino-search-2025` | ATLAS Collaboration, EPJC 85 (2025) 1437, DOI 10.1140/epjc/s10052-025-14957-6. Search for RPC squark/gluino pair-production simplified models in tau(s)+jets+missing-transverse-momentum: 140 fb⁻¹ at 13 TeV (2015–18) plus 51.8 fb⁻¹ at 13.6 TeV (2022–23); three tau/lepton channels; independently optimized cut-and-count and ML strategies combined in profile-likelihood fits. Paper reports no significant overall deviation, observed 95% CLs exclusions reaching gluino 2.25 TeV / squark 1.7 TeV only in its specific simplified mass parameterizations. | The paper states model-specific spectra, branching fractions, narrow-width approximation, R-parity conservation, detector simulations, trigger selection, tau fake-factor background, jet/tau/lepton calibrations, correlated/uncorrelated nuisances, luminosity uncertainties, and asymptotic profile-likelihood validation. It also reports a locally most-compatible ML benchmark with `p_local=0.00058` (`Z=3.2`) while concluding there is no significant overall deviation; retain this local/background-fit detail and do not turn a local value or contour into a universal SUSY exclusion. Mass limits do not apply to all SUSY models or spectra. |

For the ATLAS tau search, the full 47-page published PDF was available. I read the abstract, model parameterization, detector and dataset descriptions, reconstruction/calibration and trigger excerpts, statistical model/systematics, results and conclusion; I did not independently inspect the raw collision data, auxiliary likelihood files, or every cited detector-performance paper. For the 2026 ATLAS EFT combination, the 65-page arXiv PDF was available and I read its abstract, input-analysis inventory, event-overlap treatment, SMEFT parameterization, likelihood/systematics, confidence-interval method, and results. I did not reprocess the HEPData or audit every original measurement's detector calibration. For the CMS 2026 combination, the 79-page v2 paper was available; I read the input list, operator parameterization, systematic-correlation section, likelihood/result summary and caveats, not the raw event samples or all original channel analyses. For CMS-PAS-SMP-25-002, only the official CERN/CMS public result page was retrievable during this pass; the full PAS remains an explicit source-retrieval obligation. These are research notes, not an independent re-analysis.

## Latest-source boundary and source records

The source check establishes that the ATLAS global EFT paper was publicly available as arXiv:2604.21670v2 on 10 June 2026 and published in July 2026; CMS published its seven-measurement global SMEFT interpretation in 2026; CMS posted PAS SMP-25-002 on 30 July 2026; ATLAS public news lists SUSY summary plots updated March 2026. The last plot document could not be extracted from CERN CDS in this pass, so it contributes only to the update inventory, not a quantitative claim. I make no claim about papers/results released after this checked frontier.

| Source | Evidence/locator used here | Reading limit |
|---|---|---|
| M. Neubert, “Effective Field Theory and Heavy Quark Physics,” arXiv:hep-ph/0512222 | §2, pp. 3–5 / equations (2)–(10) as listed by the existing heavy-field suppliers; direct PDF check includes high-mode integration, low-energy locality expansion and operator sum. | Selected EFT section and matching examples; not a complete proof of a general interacting Wilsonian construction. |
| C. P. Burgess, “Introduction to Effective Field Theory,” arXiv:hep-th/0701053 | §2.2 and §§2.2.1–2.2.5, printed pp. 20–27: Wilson action from a high/low mode split, scale cancellation, matching example, local field redefinition/EOM redundancy. | Selected sections of the 55-page review; its discussion is pedagogical, not the local proof of the library's proposed theorems. |
| B. Grzadkowski et al., “Dimension-Six Terms in the Standard Model Lagrangian,” arXiv:1008.4884 | Abstract and §1, pp. 1–2 inspected: dimension-six basis, gauge-symmetry assumptions, redundant EOM combinations, and 59 structures before flavor/conjugation conventions. | The detailed §3/§5–§7 basis derivations were not reconstructed in this note; author/reviewer must check the precise table and conventions before adopting a count. |
| E. E. Jenkins, A. V. Manohar, M. Trott, SMEFT RGE I–III, arXiv:1308.2627, 1310.4838, 1312.2014 | Titles/abstracts and bibliographic records inspected; I–III divide scalar, Yukawa, and gauge terms of one-loop dimension-six anomalous dimensions. | Matrices and scheme conventions not independently checked here; source candidate only until exact sector, flavor and normalization are verified. |
| G. Buchalla, O. Catà, C. Krause, “Complete Electroweak Chiral Lagrangian with a Light Higgs at NLO,” arXiv:1307.5017v3 | Abstract/introduction, pp. 1–2: nonlinear Goldstone coset, singlet light scalar, loop/chiral power counting, one-loop counterterms and NLO basis. | Detailed basis tables and coefficient conventions require focused read before local reuse. |
| A. Adams et al., “Causality, Analyticity and an IR Obstruction to UV Completion,” arXiv:hep-th/0602178 | Abstract, forward-amplitude argument around §4 / pp. 14–20, and stated caveat near pp. 17–18: positivity follows under analyticity and high-energy growth assumptions; mass gap/IR regulator helps control the Froissart bound and massless cuts. | Their result is a specified shift-symmetric scalar example and related applications, not a universal theorem for every EFT, gauge amplitude, or gravity. |
| J. Tokuda, K. Aoki, S. Hirano, “Gravitational Positivity Bounds,” arXiv:2007.15009v2 | Abstract and sections 2–3 inventory inspected: graviton `t`-channel pole, Regge assumption, finite correction and possible controlled positivity violation. | No universal gravitational positivity theorem is claimed; exact bounds depend on Regge and IR assumptions. |
| L. Buoninfante, L.-Q. Shao, A. Tokareva, “Non-local positivity bounds: islands in Terra Incognita,” arXiv:2412.08634v2 (19 Feb 2025) | Abstract and introduction, pp. 1–2: polynomial boundedness is an explicit locality-related assumption; exponential boundedness yields different bounds. | Source is a research paper on a broadened UV class, not a consensus replacement for local-QFT bounds. |
| S. Weinberg, “Non-Renormalization Theorems in Non-Renormalizable Theories,” arXiv:hep-th/9803099 | Abstract and beginning of proof, pp. 1–3: perturbative Wilsonian supersymmetric theorem with a cutoff preserving supersymmetry/gauge invariance and holomorphic `F(Phi,W)`. | Only its stated perturbative Wilsonian claim is recorded. No nonperturbative, 1PI, or generic running conclusion is imported. |
| ATLAS Collaboration, EFT combination, arXiv:2604.21670v2 | Direct PDF sections 2–5, with the abstract, input tables, likelihood, systematics, coefficient fits and results described above. | No raw events or independent likelihood reproduction. |
| CMS Collaboration, CMS-SMP-24-003, arXiv:2504.02958v2 / EPJC 86 (2026) 331 | Direct 79-page PDF: combined inputs, SMEFT parameterization, systematic correlation, PCA, fit compatibility and source caveats. | No raw events or full re-analysis of seven component likelihoods. |
| CMS Collaboration, CMS-PAS-SMP-25-002 (30 Jul 2026) | Official result page abstract, public result summary, operator/channel list, reweighting method and binned-profile-likelihood description. | PAS PDF inaccessible during this pass; no numeric coefficient limits are copied. |
