The interpretation adopts $R$-parity-conserving simplified models, where $R=(-1)^{3(B-L)+2s}$ is a conserved $Z_2$ quantum number, $B$ and $L$ denote baryon and lepton number, $s$ denotes spin, Standard Model particles are even, and their superpartners are odd. The gluino may decay through a virtual squark; squark decays proceed through the lightest chargino or next-to-lightest neutralino. The tau-slepton and tau-sneutrino are co-next-to-lightest supersymmetric particles. The lightest neutralino is assumed bino-like, meaning predominantly the superpartner of the hypercharge gauge boson, and the lightest chargino and next-to-lightest neutralino are wino-like, meaning predominantly superpartners of weak gauge bosons. The source fixes
$$m(\tilde\chi_1^\pm)=m(\tilde\chi_2^0)=\frac{m(\tilde g\text{ or }\tilde q)+m(\tilde\chi_1^0)}{2},\qquad m(\tilde\tau)=m(\tilde\nu_\tau)=\frac{m(\tilde\chi_1^\pm)+m(\tilde\chi_1^0)}{2}.$$
The chargino and next-to-lightest-neutralino branching fractions to tau-slepton or tau-sneutrino modes are set to $50\%$, other supersymmetric couplings are set to zero, and the narrow-width approximation treats intermediate resonances as on-shell because their widths are assumed small relative to their masses. These choices define the reported simplified-model contours.

## Setup references

[[def-cm-measurement-model|Measurement, operational observable, and calibration model]]

[ATLAS tau search, model and dataset sections](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1140/epjc/s10052-025-14957-6)

## Procedure

items/exp-aqft-cms-boosted-susy-search-2025.md:64:CMS-PAS-SUS-23-014 reports a search using proton-proton collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=13\) TeV from 2016–2018, with integrated luminosities 36.3, 41.5, and 59.8 fb\(^{-1}\) in the three years, totaling 138 fb\(^{-1}\). CMS is a general-purpose detector with a superconducting solenoid; a silicon tracker and electromagnetic and hadronic calorimeters are inside the magnet, and muon detectors are outside it. A two-level trigger system selects events for storage. The search targets highly boosted top quarks, W, Z, or Higgs bosons reconstructed from hadronic decay products, or boosted leptonic jets. It uses zero-lepton, one-isolated-lepton, and one-nonisolated-lepton channels. A boosted object is one whose large momentum makes its decay products collimated in the detector. The analysis interprets data in specified simplified supersymmetry models with pair-produced gluinos, top squarks, bottom squarks, or electroweakinos, including both R-parity-conserving and R-parity-violating decay chains.
items/exp-aqft-cms-boosted-susy-search-2025.md:76:The analysis reconstructs particles with the CMS particle-flow method and forms anti-\(k_T\) jets with radius parameter \(R=0.4\). For each event it forms two “megajets” from the selected final-state objects and calculates the source-defined razor variables \(M_R\) and \(R^2\) from those megajets and the missing transverse momentum. These variables are used with lepton count, jet count, b-tagged-jet count, and boosted-object tags to define exclusive signal regions. For each dominant background, data-over-simulation correction factors are obtained from background-enriched control regions using simultaneous fits. The factors are applied to simulated background events in signal regions, and validation regions with similar but distinct kinematics check the modeling. “Control region” means an event selection used to constrain a background estimate; “validation region” is a separate selection used to test that estimate.
items/exp-aqft-cms-boosted-susy-search-2025.md:78:In the signal regions CMS performs a binned maximum-likelihood fit to templates of the signal and backgrounds, using both event-count normalization and the binned shape information. Systematic uncertainties are nuisance parameters with probability constraints; correlations across processes, regions, and data-taking years are propagated. Signal cross-section upper limits use the collaboration's asymptotic CL\(_s\) procedure, with \(CL_s=CL_{s+b}/CL_b\); a tested signal point is excluded at the reported 95% confidence level when \(CL_s<0.05\). Expected limits are computed from the background-only test-statistic distribution. This is the PAS's reported inference procedure, not an independent fit or likelihood reconstruction.
items/exp-aqft-cms-boosted-susy-search-2025.md:82:[CMS full PAS, §§2, 5–10: razor variables, object selection, background estimate, fit, and CL\(_s\) procedure](https://inspirehep.net/files/be754b4c35f11643a9b6f4cabf36deaa).
items/exp-aqft-cms-boosted-susy-search-2025.md:102:The PAS reports correction-factor uncertainties of 10–50% for backgrounds, with a comparable effect from the \(Z\to\nu\bar\nu\)+jets estimate. Jet-energy, jet-resolution, trigger, prefiring, and pileup uncertainties each contribute at the percent level or below; lepton scale-factor effects are at the few-percent level; b-tagging effects are about 5%; boosted-object tagging and mistagging effects are 5–15%; Higgs-tagging effects are 10–20% in Higgs final states; and theory uncertainties vary from 5–30% by process. These reported ranges are inputs to the collaboration's fit, not a reanalysis. The likelihood retains correlations for sources shared across bins and the relevant processes and years.
items/exp-aqft-cms-boosted-susy-search-2025.md:112:The listed mass reaches apply to the PAS's named signal models, production modes, branching patterns, mass planes, and event selections. They are not a framework-wide exclusion of supersymmetry and do not establish a supersymmetry-breaking vacuum. The collaboration reports a CL\(_s\) exclusion procedure; a 95% confidence-level limit is not a posterior probability that a model is true or false. No collision events, detector corrections, nuisance correlations, or limits are independently reconstructed here.
items/exp-aqft-atlas-compressed-higgsino-search-2026.md:60:ATLAS searched for electroweak production of higgsino-like states using 140 fb\(^{-1}\) of \(pp\) collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=13\) TeV recorded from 2015 through 2018. A higgsino is the fermionic superpartner of a Higgs field. In the simplified signal model, the lightest neutralino \(\tilde\chi^0_1\) is the lightest supersymmetric particle (LSP), and the chargino \(\tilde\chi^\pm_1\) and next neutralino \(\tilde\chi^0_2\) are nearby in mass. “Compressed” means their mass differences are small compared with their masses. The mass splitting is \(\Delta m=m(\tilde\chi^\pm_1)-m(\tilde\chi^0_1)\); the model assumes \(m(\tilde\chi^0_2)-m(\tilde\chi^0_1)=2\Delta m\). The ATLAS apparatus includes an inner tracking detector, solenoid, electromagnetic and hadronic calorimeters, and a muon spectrometer, with event selection initiated by missing-transverse-momentum triggers. Missing transverse momentum is the negative vector sum of reconstructed transverse momenta; its magnitude is denoted \(E_T^{\rm miss}\). An initial-state-radiation (ISR) jet is a high-momentum jet emitted with the produced higgsino pair before their decays; its recoil helps make the invisible pair's missing transverse momentum triggerable.
items/exp-aqft-atlas-compressed-higgsino-search-2026.md:70:Two non-overlapping strategies target different splittings. The displaced-track search targets \(\Delta m\) about 0.3–1 GeV, where a chargino can travel roughly 0.1–10 mm before decaying to a low-momentum pion. It selects events with \(E_T^{\rm miss}>275\) GeV, a central leading jet with \(p_T>250\) GeV, one to four jets above 30 GeV, and azimuthal separation \(\Delta\phi>0.4\) between each selected jet and the missing-momentum direction. Candidate charged tracks have \(p_T<5\) GeV and a sizeable transverse impact parameter \(d_0\), the shortest transverse-plane distance from the track to the beam line. Event-level and track-level neural-network scores define signal regions. The one-lepton-plus-one-track (1\(\ell\)1T) search targets \(\Delta m\) about 1–3 GeV, with the simplified-model relation above. It requires \(E_T^{\rm miss}>200\) GeV, a jet with \(p_T>100\) GeV, no b-tagged jets, exactly one low-momentum electron or muon, and a second low-momentum track; the lepton threshold is 4.5–10 GeV for electrons or 3–10 GeV for muons, and track thresholds are channel-specific. Neural networks separate signal-like configurations. Background estimates combine simulation with control-region and data-driven estimates, followed by validation in separate regions.
items/exp-aqft-atlas-compressed-higgsino-search-2026.md:72:For the reported comparisons, ATLAS fits data and Standard Model background predictions with a profile-likelihood method: nuisance parameters for systematic effects are constrained by Gaussian terms, and their reported correlations are retained. The collaboration uses the CL\(_s\) hypothesis-testing procedure with its asymptotic approximation; a signal point is excluded when the reported CL\(_s\) value is below 0.05. “Observed” limits use collision data, while “expected” limits use the background-only model. This item reports that procedure and does not reconstruct its likelihood or nuisance covariance.
items/exp-aqft-atlas-compressed-higgsino-search-2026.md:86:The collaboration reports no significant excess over the Standard Model prediction in either search; all signal-region deviations are below 2 standard deviations. The observed displaced-track exclusion reaches about \(m(\tilde\chi^\pm_1)=200\) GeV at \(\Delta m=0.6\) GeV, and the one-lepton-plus-one-track exclusion reaches \(m(\tilde\chi^\pm_1)=130\) GeV at \(\Delta m=2\) GeV. Combining the searches, ATLAS reports exclusion of chargino masses below 126 GeV at 95% confidence level for \(\Delta m\) from 0.3 to 2 GeV in the stated simplified higgsino model. These are collaboration-reported statistical inferences conditional on the analysis and model.
items/exp-aqft-atlas-compressed-higgsino-search-2026.md:96:The reported integrated-luminosity uncertainty is 0.83%. The fit includes statistical, detector, background-estimation, and signal-model uncertainties as Gaussian-constrained nuisance parameters and retains the collaboration's correlations. The paper reports signal-region pulls below 2 standard deviations, with the largest listed deviations being −1.3 and +1.5 standard deviations in separate regions. This summary does not recompute those pulls, efficiencies, systematic variations, or the asymptotic CL\(_s\) limits.
